{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "It minimizes the significance of the decision by focusing only on short-term travel improvements.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text explicitly emphasizes long-term consequences—identity, environment, and economic direction—not just short-term convenience. The ending directly contradicts this by framing the decision as transformative, not trivial."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It downplays the community’s role by suggesting the outcome is inevitable regardless of their choice.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text repeatedly stresses that the decision is up to the islanders and will 'shape the Isle of Meridian for many years to come,' clearly affirming their agency, not inevitability."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It dismisses environmental concerns by implying that economic growth outweighs ecological risks.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text presents environmental and cultural concerns as equally valid and central to the debate. The ending reinforces that the decision involves balancing these values, not dismissing them."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It reduces the decision to a simple trade-off between two equally flawed options.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text suggests a middle ground is possible and worth exploring, showing that the options aren’t mutually exclusive or equally bad—contradicting the idea that both are flawed."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It fails to address the economic benefits, making the decision seem purely symbolic.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text thoroughly discusses economic growth, jobs, and property values in favor of the bridge, and the ending ties these concrete impacts to long-term identity and future direction—far from ignoring economic factors."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "It implies that the island’s past is irrelevant to its future development.",  
      "why_wrong": "The ending explicitly contrasts embracing the future with holding onto the past, showing that the island’s history and identity are central to the decision—directly contradicting the idea that the past is irrelevant."  
    }  
  ]  
}
